---

<Layout
  title={`Manufacturing Industries | CBSE Class 10 Geography | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Geography Chapter 6 study notes on Manufacturing Industries. Importance of manufacturing, industry-agriculture linkages, location factors, classification of industries, agro-based (textile, sugar) & mineral-based (iron & steel, aluminum, chemical, fertilizer, cement, automobile, IT), environmental degradation and pollution control with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Manufacturing Industries
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Geography Chapter 6</span>
      <h1>Manufacturing Industries</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Explore secondary production: manufacturing as the backbone of economic development, industrial location geography, heavy and light industrial sectors, pollution impacts, and sustainable industrial effluent treatment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Industrial Economics, Classifications &amp; Pollution Control</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏭 Importance of Manufacturing &amp; Location</h3>
          <p class="formula">Economic Strength = Development of Manufacturing</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Definition:</strong> Production of goods in large quantities after processing raw materials to more valuable products is called manufacturing.</li>
            <li><strong>Why is Manufacturing the Backbone of Development?</strong>
              <br/>• Modernizes agriculture (tractors, pumps, fertilizers).
              <br/>• Reduces heavy dependence of people on agricultural income by creating jobs in secondary/tertiary sectors.
              <br/>• Eradicates unemployment and poverty.
              <br/>• Expands trade and exports, bringing valuable foreign exchange.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Industrial Location Factors:</strong> Raw material availability, cheap power, skilled/unskilled labour, capital, banking/market proximity, and government policies.</li>
            <li><strong>Agglomeration Economies:</strong> Many industries come together in urban centers to make use of advantages offered by urban institutions (banking, transport, labor, consulting).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧺 Agro-Based Industries: Textiles &amp; Sugar</h3>
          <p class="formula">Cotton, Jute, Sugar Mills</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Cotton Textiles:</strong> Concentrated in Maharashtra and Gujarat due to black soil cotton belt, humid climate (prevents yarn breakage), proximity to Mumbai port, cheap labour, and market. Today, spinning remains centralized while weaving is decentralized to incorporate traditional designs.</li>
            <li><strong>Jute Textiles:</strong> Located mainly in <strong>West Bengal along the banks of the Hugli River</strong>: proximity to raw jute fields, abundant water for processing/retting, cheap water transport, railways/road network, and inexpensive labor from Bihar/WB.</li>
            <li><strong>Sugar Industry:</strong> Seasonal industry; raw material (sugarcane) is bulky and its sucrose content decreases with haulage time.
              <br/>• <em>Shift to South/Western States (Maharashtra):</em> Cane produced here has higher sucrose content, cooler climate ensures longer crushing season, and cooperative sugar mills are highly successful.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚙️ Mineral-Based Industries</h3>
          <p class="formula">Iron &amp; Steel, Aluminum, Chemical, Fertilizer, IT</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Iron and Steel Industry (Basic Industry):</strong> Heavy industry using bulky raw materials: Iron ore, coking coal, and limestone in the ratio of roughly <strong>4 : 2 : 1</strong> plus manganese to harden steel.
              <br/>• Concentrated in <strong>Chotanagpur plateau</strong> due to low-cost iron ore, high-grade coking coal nearby, and cheap labor.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Aluminium Smelting:</strong> 2nd most important metallurgical industry in India. Light, resistant to corrosion, good conductor of heat. Requires regular supply of electricity and assured raw material (bauxite) at minimum cost. (Odisha, WB, Kerala, UP, Chhattisgarh, TN).</li>
            <li><strong>Chemical Industry:</strong> Inorganic (sulfuric acid, nitric acid, soda ash, caustic soda) and Organic (petrochemicals for synthetic fibers, plastics, rubber).</li>
            <li><strong>Information Technology &amp; Electronics:</strong> Produces televisions, telephones, radars, computers. <strong>Bengaluru is the electronic capital of India</strong>. Generates massive female employment (over 30%).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>☣️ Environmental Degradation &amp; Control</h3>
          <p class="formula">4 Types of Pollution &amp; NTPC Sustainable Model</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Air Pollution:</strong> High proportion of undesirable gases: sulfur dioxide and carbon monoxide. Particulate matter emitted by smokestacks without electrostatic precipitators.</li>
            <li><strong>2. Water Pollution:</strong> Organic and inorganic industrial wastes and effluents discharged into rivers (paper, pulp, chemical, textile, dyeing, tanneries).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Thermal Pollution:</strong> Hot water from factories and thermal plants drained into rivers before cooling, killing aquatic life.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Noise Pollution:</strong> Industrial machinery, generators, drills causing hearing impairment, stress, and physiological disorders.</li>
            <li><strong>Treatment of Industrial Effluents:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Primary treatment:</em> Mechanical means (screening, sedimentation).
              <br/>• <em>Secondary treatment:</em> Biological processes (aerobic digestion).
              <br/>• <em>Tertiary treatment:</em> Biological, chemical, and physical recycling of wastewater.
            </li>
            <li><strong>NTPC ISO 14001 Certification:</strong> Proactive approach: modern equipment adoption, ecological monitoring, ash water recycling, green belts, and liquid waste management.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Iron &amp; Steel Inputs Ratio &amp; 3-Stage Effluent Treatment</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · Raw Material Proportions in Steel Making</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Steel making inputs ratio">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Input 1: Iron Ore (4 parts) -->
              <rect x="30" y="50" width="90" height="130" fill="#1e293b" rx="6"/>
